# nh — Design Notes & Decisions

A new Go CLI (`nh`) for NervesCloud / NervesHub, replacing the Elixir +
Burrito `nerves_hub_cli`. This file captures the reasoning and the foundation
so it can be used as context (e.g. @-mention it in Zed's Agent Panel).

## Decision: write the new CLI in Go

Chosen over Rust, Zig, and staying on Elixir.

**Why not stay on Elixir + Burrito**
- The BEAM is built for long-lived, supervised, concurrent processes (OTP,
  hot code reload, the actor model). A CLI boots, does one thing, and exits —
  it uses none of that while still paying for VM startup on every invocation
  and bundling the whole runtime into a large binary.
- The current mess is largely a migration artifact: it began as a library of
  Mix tasks and was reshaped into a standalone CLI. Cleaning that up in Elixir
  is effort spent refactoring toward a CLI shape the language gives no native
  scaffolding for. Elixir has no opinionated CLI framework (no Cobra/clap), so
  command organization drifts toward ad hoc.

**Why Go over Rust**
- *Crypto is the deciding axis.* The CLI generates X.509 device certificates
  and handles signing keys. Go's `crypto/x509`, `crypto/ed25519`, and
  `crypto/tls` are batteries-included and battle-tested. In Rust you assemble
  that layer (rcgen, ed25519-dalek, rustls/openssl), and X.509 *generation*
  specifically is the least turnkey part of the Rust crypto story. This is the
  security-sensitive code least suited to hand-wiring.
- *Contributors.* The Nerves community is Elixir end to end. Go is far more
  approachable for a drive-by contributor (days, not months) than Rust's
  borrow checker.
- *Native tongue of the category.* Infra/devops CLIs are overwhelmingly Go —
  including flyctl, already used to deploy NervesHub. Recognizable idioms,
  mature libs (Cobra), well-trodden cross-compile + release tooling.
- *Time to value.* Faster to a clean, cross-compiled binary; simpler release
  pipeline. A REST + crypto + arg-parsing + output CLI has none of the hot
  paths or invariants where Rust's stricter model earns its overhead.

**Zig** was ruled out: pre-1.0 with breaking changes, young `std.http`, no
mature arg-parsing framework, and no high-level X.509 generation in std
(you'd hand-roll DER/ASN.1). Note Burrito already uses Zig internally for
cross-compilation — that's Zig's actual sweet spot, not application code.

> A prior Rust experiment (`nervesctl`, clap/reqwest/serde/tabled) is treated
> as a throwaway prototype — not a reason to pick Rust via sunk cost.

## Migration principle

Treat the existing CLI's behavior — especially the crypto / cert-generation /
signing-key paths — as the executable spec. Port command-by-command behind the
same `nh`-style UX so users don't notice. Do **not** rebuild the
security-sensitive parts from scratch; lean on Go's `crypto/x509` so that layer
isn't hand-assembled.

## Project foundation (Cobra + cobra-cli)

```sh
mkdir nh && cd nh
go mod init github.com/nerves-hub/nh   # adjust to the real repo path
go install github.com/spf13/cobra-cli@latest

cobra-cli init --author "Your Name" --license apache
```

Top-level nouns (mirroring the existing CLI):

```sh
cobra-cli add config
cobra-cli add user
cobra-cli add org
cobra-cli add product
cobra-cli add device
cobra-cli add firmware
cobra-cli add deployment
cobra-cli add key        # firmware signing keys
```

Verbs attach to a parent via `-p <noun>Cmd`:

```sh
cobra-cli add auth -p userCmd          # nh user auth
cobra-cli add whoami -p userCmd        # nh user whoami
cobra-cli add list -p deviceCmd        # nh device list
cobra-cli add create -p deviceCmd      # nh device create
cobra-cli add cert -p deviceCmd        # nh device cert ...
cobra-cli add set -p configCmd         # nh config set
cobra-cli add get -p configCmd         # nh config get
```

## Architecture: keep `cmd/` thin

The thing the old codebase lost in the library-to-CLI migration. Be deliberate
from line one.

- `cmd/` — flag parsing only; calls into `internal/`. No business logic.
- `internal/api` — HTTP client (`net/http` + structs). The reqwest-equivalent.
- `internal/config` — URI / token / data-dir handling; bind env vars
  (`NERVES_CLOUD_TOKEN` / `NERVES_HUB_TOKEN`, `_ORG`, `_PRODUCT`, `_URI`,
  `_NON_INTERACTIVE`, etc.).
- `internal/pki` (or `crypto`) — wraps `crypto/x509` and `crypto/ed25519` for
  cert and signing-key paths.

```sh
mkdir -p internal/api internal/config internal/pki
```

## Distribution

The old CLI ships via a Homebrew tap + curl installer with cross-compiled
binaries (what Burrito handled). Replace with **GoReleaser**: cross-compilation,
GitHub releases, and the Homebrew tap formula from one `.goreleaser.yaml`. Add
it early rather than hand-rolling release scripts.

